%[text] %[text:anchor:H_FA828509] ## 5. Tune suspension damping using the mass-spring-damper model
%[text] Now that you've solved the ODE in Simulink, you can apply it to tune the suspension of a vehicle modeled as a single mass-spring-damper. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Schematic shock absorber on car](text:image:0c79)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Schematic of the mass-spring-damper model applied to a vehicle. The entire suspension has a stiffness,* $k${"altText":"k"}*, and damping,* $c${"altText":"c"}*. At rest, the vertical position of the vehicle's undercarriage is* $x=0${"altText":"x=0"}. 
%[text] Your goal in this section is to tune the suspension so that it meets design requirements. 
%[text] ![write.png](text:image:19ad) **Exercise.** Consider a vehicle with the following properties:
%[text] - Vehicle mass: 1360 kg
%[text] - Suspension stiffness: 26,000 N/m \
%[text] Your task is to select a damping, $c${"altText":"c"}, that meets the following design requirements. After encountering a bump in the road, which is modeled by a 9000 N force applied for 0.1 seconds, the vehicle should:
%[text] 1. have a maximum absolute vertical displacement of less than 0.1 m after impact,
%[text] 2. after 1 second, the absolute vertical displacement should be less than 0.02 m, and
%[text] 3. the damping coefficient should be as small as possible while still meeting requirements 1 and 2. \
%[text] Assume that, initially, the vehicle has no motion or displacement in the vertical direction.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Schematic of requirement for shock absorber](text:image:6a7d)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Schematic of the design requirements. The initial vertical displacement should be less than 0.1 m. Subsequent oscillations should have a magnitude of less than 0.02 m.*
%[text] ![interact.png](text:image:586b) **Tasks.** To adjust the design so that it meets the requirements, perform the following.
%[text] 1. Open the model [msdTune.slx](<matlab: open_system('Models/msdTune')>). This model has a masked subsystem containing the mass-spring-damper model you worked with in the previous section. You can look under the mask by clicking the arrow (![removeFromQab.png](text:image:83a4)) to view this model.
%[text] 2. Double-click the [mask](<matlab: hilite_system('msdTune/mass-spring-damper model'); pause(1); hilite_system('msdTune/mass-spring-damper model','none');>) of the model and set the initial conditions of the displacement $x(0)${"altText":"x(0)"} and velocity $\\dot{x}(0)${"altText":"\dot{x}(0)"}.
%[text] 3. Set the fixed parameters of the model: [k](<matlab: hilite_system('msdTune/k'); pause(1); hilite_system('msdTune/k','none');>) and [m](<matlab: hilite_system('msdTune/m'); pause(1); hilite_system('msdTune/m','none');>). The model already applies the correct impulse force $F${"altText":"F"}, so do not modify the "F" subsystem.
%[text] 4. Set a damping coefficient, [c](<matlab: hilite_system('msdTune/c'); pause(1); hilite_system('msdTune/c','none');>), and run the model. View the vehicle's bouncing behavior in the [scope](<matlab: hilite_system('msdTune/Scope'); pause(1); hilite_system('msdTune/Scope','none');>).
%[text] 5. Tune the damping until all three requirements have been met. \
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![decorative](text:image:65f7)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Schematic of the Simulink model and where you should perform the tasks*
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A5E03AB0] ### ![compass\_edit.png](text:image:0a0e)**Further exploration**
%[text] In reality, a vehicle's suspension system is more complicated than a single mass-spring-damper. An uncomplicated improvement to this model uses two mass-spring-dampers: one for the front suspension and one for the rear. To learn more about this approach, you can review the [automotive suspension](https://www.mathworks.com/help/simulink/slref/automotive-suspension.html) application in the Simulink documentation.
